{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Test evidence — final, and a correction to my previous comment's \"result to follow\"\n\nMy earlier comment said a broad `tests/interfaces tests/unit` sweep \"was still running; result to follow\".\n**That sweep never produced a result** — it was killed by the harness. Worse, the two background waiters I\nhad armed used `until ! pgrep -f \"pytest tests/interfaces tests/unit\"`, a pattern that **matched each\nwaiter's own command line**, so they could never exit and my status checks were reading my own waiters\nrather than the suite. I reported \"still running\" for roughly an hour after the real run was already dead.\nBoth waiters are killed and reaped. Recording this because the previous comment's claim was not merely\nincomplete, it was measuring the wrong thing.\n\nRe-run properly, logging to plain files instead of through `tail` (where nothing surfaces until exit):\n\n| suite | result |\n|---|---|\n| `tests/interfaces` (**complete tier**, 2,182 collected) | **2172 passed, 10 skipped** — exit 0, 19:24 |\n| `tests/unit` blast radius — `-k \"canonical or hlc or timestamp or mcp or schema or types or json or serial or signing or opcert\"` | **1073 passed, 6 skipped** (12,956 deselected), 3:33 |\n| `tests/unit/test_canonical.py` (explicit) | **6 passed** — includes `test_large_timestamp_round_trips_exactly`, rebar's pinned >2^53 regression test |\n| held-out regression file | **5 passed** |\n| `make lint` | exit 0; complexity ratchet `active=40 new=0 increased=0 stale=9` |\n| `make typecheck` | **Success: no issues found in 677 source files** |\n| `check_mcp_models --check` | OK — no missing declarations |\n| `types.py` vs generator | byte-identical (no hand-edit drift) |\n\n**What I deliberately did NOT run, and why.** The remaining ~12,956 unit tests (store internals, LLM prompt\nlogic, workflows) were left unrun. The full unit tier projected to **~2 hours** at the observed rate, on a\nbox concurrently running three other sessions' suites. This change is confined to the MCP tool-return\nboundary plus four schema files and a regenerated `types.py`; `tests/interfaces` is the tier that exercises\nthose surfaces and it is **fully green**, and the unit-tier selection above covers canonical JSON, HLC\ntimestamps, schemas, types, signing and op-certs. Spending two hours of shared CPU on store and LLM tests\nthat cannot reach the changed code was not proportionate. Stating the trade-off rather than implying full\ncoverage.\n\n### Residual risk — one observed fragility, disposition: ACCEPTED (not a defect)\n\nWhile sweeping for leaked processes I found an orphaned `rebar._snapshot.gc_trigger.run_detached` helper\n(PPID 1, 52.7% CPU, working on a `pytest-of-joeoakhart/.../repo` temp dir from my own finished test run). I\nreaped it. Before filing it as a leak I read the code: `src/rebar/_snapshot/gc_trigger.py:185-215` shows\n`run_detached` acquires a worker lock, runs **one** `janitor.run_gc` pass, and returns. It is a bounded\nsingle-pass workload that detaches deliberately; it terminates on its own. That is **not** the unbounded-loop\nhazard `AGENTS.md` targets, so **no ticket is filed** — my kill was precautionary cleanup, not a fix. Noting\nit so a future reader who sees the same PPID-1 process does not re-open it as a leak.\n\nFinal state: worktree clean at `afe5e3551485`, no processes of mine remaining, other sessions' suites\nuntouched and verified still running."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787865488775987644,"uuid":"2ae97dcc-f51d-4e31-a4a1-741646422a22"}